<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The carriage pull ~ one dial per (peer, Nexus), through a gate

A carriage reads a peer's public map through a gate and nowhere else: the bare route stands SHUT by name,
and what the house named to open carriage peering is a proven gate-native caller (the shut-routes house ruling, stable-founding-road #/rulings). This is that caller: the carriage cap pulls through `gateNativeCarriagePull`, and no bare
path stands beside it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/where-the-pin-comes-from`>>

!! Where the pin comes from ~ never the wire, never the dial entry

The pair index (`admit-gate-pin`), keyed (peer's key, Nexus hex, `/flow`, `floor`), filled only through an
authenticated relation or a carried signed pin set (house ruling "The admit's gate pin"; `place-floor-pin`).
A peer this vessel holds no pin for at any Nexus it stands in is not dialed at all (`pin-absent`): a
stranger's crossroads, and a crossroads of another Nexus, are never knocked on. The `refresh` hook runs once
before each pull: where the vessel files the pins its boards carried since the last one.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-it-presents`>>

!! What it presents ~ the caller's own standing

The caller's `presentationFor(nexus, pin)`: a face with its admit at that Nexus, or a place with its
carriage contract there (`herm-carriage` decides which). A Nexus at which this vessel can present nothing is
skipped by name (`presentation-absent`), never dialed bare. Nothing is composed on the wire's behalf.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/feed-or-fade`>>

!! Feed or fade

The first verified pull wins (`pullAndVerifyFlow`, the flow pointer bound to the publisher's key); every Nexus
is tried at most once per pull; nothing throws. The last refusal names itself when none answered.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/edges`>>

